<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mont-Terrible</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1793–1800</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mont-Terrible was a French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> created on 23 March 1793 from the territory of the Rauracian Republic, the former northern lands of the Prince-Bishopric of Basel in the Jura. Named after a mountain near its capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porrentruy">Porrentruy</a>, it was too small to survive and was merged into the department of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haut-rhin">Haut-Rhin</a> in 1800. In 1815 most of its territory was given to the Swiss canton of Bern.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mont-terrible.jpg" alt="Mont-Terrible" width="320" height="257" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Starus · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:D%C3%A9partement_du_Mont-Terrible.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
